;;; A wildcard `_` as a pattern's relation head stopped the run with a Python
;;; spelling — **W20**, register row `W20/wild-in-head`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds a)`, and one rule that concludes `(hit w)` from any
;;; fact whose one argument is `a`, whatever its relation: the pattern's
;;; head is the wildcard `_`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A relation is named by a name or by a parameter its activator binds
;;; (Q29), and a bare `_` is reserved and refused wherever it is written
;;; (Q-M1e2.22): the program is refused at the parse, at the `_`, and every
;;; command that reads it exits 1 (`ein test`: 2). `wild-in-head-twin.ein`
;;; writes the head out as the disjunction over the relations a fact of one
;;; argument `a` can belong to; a fresh head variable is no twin, since
;;; `(?r a)` with no parameter `?r` is refused at load as `_` is.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `c73a0796`, release and debug, every command that compiled the rule
;;; exited 1: `ein saturate` with a Python `CompileError`, *unbound relation
;;; head ?Wildcard() in a premise of `SForm(head=Wildcard(),
;;; args=(Atom(name='a'),))`* …, and `ein solve` and `ein oracle` with the
;;; message alone, the Python IR's spelling of the pattern. Today the parse
;;; refuses the `_`: *unexpected `_`: _ (reserved)*.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds a)
(rule w () :match (_ a) :assert (hit w))
